================================================================================
SPP-Valgrind: Modified Valgrind for C++ Execution Tracing
================================================================================

This is a modified version of Valgrind 3.27.0 that generates execution traces
for C++ programs. These traces capture program state at each instruction,
including stack frames, local/global variables, heap allocations, and stdout
output.

Modifications Overview
----------------------
This implementation is based on Philip Guo's (pgbovine) original modifications
to Valgrind for Online Python Tutor, which enabled execution tracing for C/C++
programs. The original work used Valgrind 3.11.0 (circa 2015).

This fork represents a complete port and modernization:
- Updated from Valgrind 3.11.0 to Valgrind 3.27.0 (2024)
- Adapted all debug info API calls to use DiEpoch for epoch-aware queries
- Added ARM64 support via CFA register mapping
- Fixed stdout capture using file descriptor redirection
- Migrated JSON library integration to use Valgrind memory functions

Key Features Added
------------------
- **Execution Trace Generation**: Captures program state at each instruction
  - Stack frame information with function names and nesting
  - Local and global variable values with type information
  - Heap memory allocations and pointer relationships
  - Program stdout output synchronized with execution steps

- **Debug Info Enhancements**:
  - Full variable name capture (removes 16-char truncation limit)
  - Variable traversal functions for locals and globals
  - JSON-formatted output for structured trace data
  - Memory definedness checking to avoid reading uninitialized data

- **Modern Compatibility**:
  - Works on Amazon Linux 2023 with ARM64 architecture
  - Compatible with GCC 11+ compiled binaries
  - Supports modern DWARF debug info formats

Modified Files
--------------
- include/pub_tool_debuginfo.h: Added traversal function declarations, JSON library
- coregrind/m_debuginfo/debuginfo.c: Variable traversal implementations
- coregrind/m_debuginfo/tytypes.c: Type printing as JSON
- coregrind/m_debuginfo/priv_tytypes.h: Internal function declarations
- memcheck/mc_main.c: Command-line options, trace file handling
- memcheck/mc_translate.c: Instrumentation to call trace function at each step
- memcheck/mc_include.h: Shared declarations for trace generation

Usage
-----
To generate a trace file:

  valgrind --tool=memcheck --source-filename=program.cpp \
           --trace-filename=/tmp/trace.vgtrace \
           ./compiled_program

The trace file contains JSON-formatted execution steps showing the complete
program state at each instruction.

License
-------
These modifications maintain the original Valgrind GPL v3+ license.
All contributed code is available under GPL version 3 or later.

For the original See++ project, see: https://github.com/knazir/SeePlusPlus

================================================================================
Original Valgrind README
================================================================================

Release notes for Valgrind
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
If you are building a binary package of Valgrind for distribution,
please read README_PACKAGERS.  It contains some important information.

If you are developing Valgrind, please read README_DEVELOPERS.  It contains
some useful information.

For instructions on how to build/install, see the end of this file.

If you have problems, consult the FAQ to see if there are workarounds.


Executive Summary
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Valgrind is a framework for building dynamic analysis tools. There are
Valgrind tools that can automatically detect many memory management
and threading bugs, and profile your programs in detail. You can also
use Valgrind to build new tools.

The Valgrind distribution currently includes seven production-quality
tools: a memory error detector, two thread error detectors, a cache
and branch-prediction profiler, a call-graph generating cache and
branch-prediction profiler, and two heap profilers. It also includes
one experimental tool: a SimPoint basic block vector generator.

Valgrind is closely tied to details of the CPU, operating system and to
a lesser extent, compiler and basic C libraries. This makes it difficult
to make it portable.  Nonetheless, it is available for the following
platforms: 

- X86/Linux
- AMD64/Linux
- PPC32/Linux
- PPC64/Linux
- ARM/Linux
- ARM64/Linux
- X86/macOS
- AMD64/macOS
- S390X/Linux
- MIPS32/Linux
- MIPS64/Linux
- nanoMIPS/Linux
- X86/Solaris
- AMD64/Solaris
- X86/FreeBSD
- AMD64/FreeBSD
- ARM64/FreeBSD

Note that AMD64 is just another name for x86_64, and Valgrind runs fine
on Intel processors.  Also note that the core of macOS is called
"Darwin" and this name is used sometimes.

Valgrind is licensed under the GNU General Public License, version 3.
Read the file COPYING in the source distribution for details.

However: if you contribute code, you need to make it available as GPL
version 3 or later, and not 3-only.


Documentation
~~~~~~~~~~~~~
A comprehensive user guide is supplied.  Point your browser at
$PREFIX/share/doc/valgrind/manual.html, where $PREFIX is whatever you
specified with --prefix= when building.


Building and installing it
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
To install from the GIT repository:

  0. Clone the code from GIT:
     git clone https://sourceware.org/git/valgrind.git
     There are further instructions at
     http://www.valgrind.org/downloads/repository.html.

  1. cd into the source directory.

  2. Run ./autogen.sh to setup the environment (you need the standard
     autoconf tools to do so).

  3. Continue with the following instructions...

To install from a tar.bz2 distribution:

  4. Run ./configure, with some options if you wish.  The only interesting
     one is the usual --prefix=/where/you/want/it/installed.

  5. Run "make".

  6. Run "make install", possibly as root if the destination permissions
     require that.

  7. See if it works.  Try "valgrind ls -l".  Either this works, or it
     bombs out with some complaint.  In that case, please let us know
     (see http://valgrind.org/support/bug_reports.html).

Important!  Do not move the valgrind installation into a place
different from that specified by --prefix at build time.  This will
cause things to break in subtle ways, mostly when Valgrind handles
fork/exec calls.


